 In the recent discussions about weapon restrictions, many bad things
have been said about "the AD&D rules."  However, most of those people
don't seem to understand the AD&D rules, which are really a lot more
reasonable than they've been made out to be here.
 The first thing is that AD&D clearly states that it's 'rules' are not
rules at all, but rather guidelines, and that the DM has ultimate
authority.  For this reason alone, it is not even *possible* to make
NetHack work like AD&D, since in NetHack real rules must be programmed
into the game, and it won't change it's mind if a reasonable argument
comes up, the way a DM would.
 However, some aspects of the AD&D rules may make sense for NetHack, so
I'll try to explain briefly... (I guarantee nothing.  *no one* can
briefly explain the AD&D rules, that's why the DM's guide weighs so
much :-)

 In AD&D, every kind of weapon has a number associated with it called
the "nonproficiency penalty."  This is roughly a measure of how hard
it is to use that weapon if you haven't learned it.  Every character
using a weapon must subtract this penalty from his rolls *unless* he
has proficiency in that weapon.  If a character has proficiency in a
weapon, this means that character has learned to use it, and can use
it without penalty (i.e. die rolls are unmodified).
  Each character class starts with a certain number of weapon
proficiencies and a rate of learning new ones.  For example, one class
may start with proficiency in 3 weapons, plus the ability to learn one
new weapon every two levels.  Wizards and such get fewer weapon
proficiencies than fighter types, naturally (though they are
compensated by a greater number of nonweapon proficiencies...).
  Therefore, when you start an AD&D character, you choose a few
weapons that your character know how to use (these are quite specific,
such as "longsword" and not just "sword").  Usually, these are the
same weapons that you buy for your starting equipment.

 The weapon *restrictions* are deliberately vague in AD&D.  For
example, it says that clerics don't use edged weapons, but doesn't say
what precisely will happen to a cleric that picks up a dagger in
combat and uses it to save himself.
 In my campaigns, weapon restirctions have always been enforced by
using proficiency.  For example, there is nothing to stop a cleric
from using an edged weapon.  But a cleric cannot start the game with
proficiency in it, and if a cleric tries to gain proficiency in an
edged weapon (which requires a lot of time and practice), this would
anger the god, who would not grant that cleric spells.  This limits
the cleric to spells of 4th level or below.
 In some cases, the AD&D rules talk specifically about what weapons to
become proficient in.  For example, a cavalier who learns a missile
weapon before learning the traditional knight's weapons (lance,
longsword, etc.) is considered a coward by his peers (and you all know
how important social class is for knights...).

 There is also something else, called weapon specialization, which
actually gives you bonuses for a weapon.  However, this is restricted
to only a few classes, and each character can only specialize in one
weapon, which takes the place of two regular proficiencies.

 Clearly, a lot of this can't apply to hack, but I'm sure the dev team
and the r.g.h readers can sift through these ideas and find what is
useful.

